WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.601
Joining us now is the former

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.736
speaker of the U.S.

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:04.604
House of Representatives, Paul Ryan.

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.472
Good to see you, sir.

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:07.140
Thanks for being here. Appreciate it.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.408
I want to get to Kevin McCarthy

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:08.842
in a moment.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.210
But first, with all this news

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.745
about classified documents,

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:12.879
whether President Biden

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:13.980
or President Trump,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:14.848
I want to just ask you,

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:15.248
as somebody

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:16.716
who when you were speaker of the House,

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:17.851
had access

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:19.052
to classified documents,

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.053
if you could like

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:22.622
give us some sort of insight into

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:25.625
how easy or not

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.326
or difficult

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:28.828
it might be to actually accidentally or.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:29.729
No, I never took them home.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.231
I left them in my safe in the office.

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:31.831
Is that right?

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:32.832
And they went from my safe

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.067
in the office down to the skiff

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.568
and then back to the safe in the office.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:38.438
I never I never took them on my body.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.473
They were in locked briefcases,

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:41.841
so I never even took them

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:42.742
out of the office.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.644
But I guess it would be different

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:45.378
if you're president.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:46.780
Maybe I'm trying to figure it out.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.847
Whether you're trying to buy it.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.782
It's the system

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.450
you put in place in your office, I guess.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.018
And you had a rigid system,

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:52.752
a rigid system

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:53.286
which they want

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:54.521
from the intelligence community

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.156
to a safe in my office,

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.724
that one cleared

00:00:57.724 --> 00:01:00.093
staffer of mine had access to,

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:01.428
we would take them from the safe

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:02.495
down to the skiff,

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:04.931
which is underground in the Capitol.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.366
I would read them

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:07.534
there, discuss them

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.202
there at my briefings.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.837
There we go back to the office,

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.505
and they'd go in that safe in either

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:13.273
the SCIF safe

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.774
or in the safe in my office,

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.709
and they would not go nowhere else.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.044
Sounds like a pretty good process.

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:19.379
That's the way we do things.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:19.712
Yeah.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:22.515
Okay, so let's talk about the House race,

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:23.883
because this hasn't happened.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:24.451
We saw something

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.251
last week

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.455
that hasn't happened in 100 years.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.522
And longer.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:30.090
If you want to go

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.891
to the number of ballots,

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:32.959
a serious battle

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:33.660
on the floor of the House

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.495
to be speaker of the House

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:36.830
and some very serious

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.198
personal criticisms

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:40.166
of the man who ultimately

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.234
won the election.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.836
Kevin McCarthy,

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:43.803
including about whether or not

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:44.604
he's trustworthy

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:45.271
and the like

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.840
as a friend of his,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.374
as somebody who was speaker when

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.243
he was House majority leader,

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:52.445
what was going through your mind

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.546
when you watched it all play out?

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:54.814
I thought he was going to gut it out.

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.850
I didn't think he'd take 15 votes.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.351
So I suppose between like vote

00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.686
four and 14,

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.888
I wasn't so sure, but I still thought

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.690
there was no alternative.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.491
So because there was

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:05.859
really no alternative.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.460
That's kind of the dynamic was different.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.761
When I was there, because I was seen

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.230
as the alternative

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.664
and I was not looking for the job.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:13.766
I sort of got drafted into it,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.269
but there was no dynamic like that

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:17.670
at play here.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.240
And Kevin had done so much more.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.241
First of all, remember,

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.143
I came midterm, so Boehner,

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.244
it was a motion to vacate

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:25.712
was being placed upon Boehner.

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.380
So I came midterm

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.649
Kevin had just worked his tail off

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.651
to campaign and fundraise

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.052
to build this majority

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.555
over two cycles, and he did that.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.789
So it's a different dynamic

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.191
than what occurred in 2015 with me,

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:38.825
which was Kevin

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.460
was the leader of the conference

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.929
as a minority leader for two cycles

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.364
built that majority

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.632
and then there really wasn't

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.400
another alternative for people to go to.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.368
So I really thought that

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.403
he would get through it

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.838
even with this type of tough margin.

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:52.205
I just didn't

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:53.239
think it take 15 votes

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.541
in all those concessions that it took

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:55.441
but it did.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:56.276
There's this moment

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:57.343
well let's just show it right now

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.078
where obviously we're Armed Services

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.413
Committee Chairman Mike Ryan.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.648
You even have to show me the picture.

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:02.482
I see.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.450
There he is lunging at Congressman

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.252
Matt Gates after Gates

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.389
did not vote I

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:10.924
thus stopping McCarthy

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:12.892
from winning on the 14th ballot.

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.460
Does this bode ill

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.329
for your party's ability

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:19.299
to unite and actually govern?

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:20.200
Look, no.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.501
I mean, these guys are get

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:22.502
that's where drugs and behind aren't

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.037
very good friends of mine.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.271
Richards, a good friend of Mike's.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:26.839
Mike just got hot under the collar.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:27.774
He just got you know,

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:28.741
they were pretty exasperated

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.709
by that time.

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:30.910
And my understanding is Matt

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.045
Gates was going to vote

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.279
for him on that 14 vote

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.781
and then changed his mind in the vote

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:36.216
without telling anybody

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:37.617
That makes people pretty upset.

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:40.587
So look hot heads happen.

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.021
Things like that occur

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.022
by the way

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.090
that's what the floor is like

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.425
when you ever have any contentious vote

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:45.825
I just know

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.060
C-SPAN usually doesn't cover that

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:47.760
because the cameras

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.895
but it's always like that

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:49.929
so should we have.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:51.097
When you have difficult votes

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:52.465
Gates wants the cameras.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:53.199
And I have to say

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.701
I know I'm not I'm

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:55.902
not a fan of their performance

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:56.703
art that has overtaken

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:58.438
our politics in the entertainment wings

00:03:58.438 --> 00:03:59.739
of both of our parties.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:00.840
This would just feed into that.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.942
So, no, I'm actually not a fan of that.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.244
But that is what the floor is

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:05.278
like when you have tough,

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:06.045
contentious votes.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.113
I've seen that movie

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:08.481
a million times over.

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:10.183
You just haven't seen it on C-SPAN.

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:10.883
And no,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.452
I don't want to see the cameras do that

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.453
because people are going to play

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.587
to the cameras

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:15.588
on the floor of Congress,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:16.422
like they play to the cameras

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.023
everywhere else

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:18.157
they do in politics these days.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:19.292
Well, the counterargument would be

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:21.027
you also see the humanity

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.595
that Republicans sitting together

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.496
will have too much entertainment

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:24.430
in politics these days.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.432
So I frankly like to see that toned down.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.000
I don't want to

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.268
add more gas to that fire.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:30.036
Such a buzz kill.

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:32.405
McCarthy made so many concessions

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:33.973
to this wing.

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:34.674
The chip Roy,

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:35.341
Matt Gates,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.242
whoever, whatever Harry

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:36.776
want to call it, these

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.578
insurgents, these rebels,

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:40.780
too.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.215
And he made these concessions

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.783
to get their votes.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:44.684
And without question,

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:45.818
the concessions

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.921
weakened the speakership and empowered

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.123
individual members.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:50.990
The motion to vacate

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:51.457
you mentioned

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:52.058
in which

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:52.925
they got rid of John

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.194
Boehner is now back to one vote

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:55.962
as it was before.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:56.763
But there are other

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:57.864
other things in terms of

00:04:57.864 --> 00:04:59.932
who was on the Rules Committee

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:02.435
opening up the process.

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:03.703
Anybody can offer a bill.

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.071
Anybody can offer amendment.

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.006
I like some of those, by the way.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.241
Some of those are pretty good.

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.109
Well, the 72 hour one is a great one.

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:11.311
The idea that you need 72 hours

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:12.545
to read a bill before you can vote

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:13.546
with the appropriations ones.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:13.980
I like that.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:14.881
So frankly,

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:15.715
I think

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:16.416
I don't want to cut you off

00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:16.916
on your question.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:18.151
But let me say this.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.253
I think I had too much power as speaker.

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:20.787
Oh, really?

00:05:20.787 --> 00:05:21.421
I really do.

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:22.221
Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:22.622
Look,

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:23.623
the thing that bothered me

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.124
the most were these omnibus,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.594
you know, a bill up that thick

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:29.662
that for people

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:30.596
ultimately decide on

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.932
15 spending bills crunched together 12

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:34.500
crunched together

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.537
and and it's thousands of pages.

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:38.571
It would be myself,

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.739
Nancy, Chuck and Mitch

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:40.306
I'm sorry

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:41.774
that House speaker

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:42.442
you know, every leader

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:43.476
you know what I'm talking about.

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:45.178
They were your friends, basically.

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:47.180
Well, some of them are ish.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.081
Yeah. Yes.

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:48.848
We all get along.

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.283
We all respect one another.

00:05:50.283 --> 00:05:51.284
The problem is no.

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:52.819
Four people should be doing all of that,

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:54.520
making those decisions.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.254
And, you know,

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:55.621
frankly,

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.557
I was making decisions on composition

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.225
of spending bills.

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:00.059
You know, I think I'm

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:01.327
a smart, principled person.

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.329
But the guy and the man,

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.664
the woman who's in the committee in

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:05.431
the subcommittee is spending

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:06.933
two years reading inspector

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:08.768
general reports, GAO reports on those.

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:10.737
They should be making those decisions,

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:12.772
not kicked up to elected leadership.

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:14.273
Are there any new rules

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.342
or rule changes that concern you

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:17.677
in terms of the free

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:18.544
for all, in terms

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.713
of whether or not what we saw last week

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:22.048
is going to be kind of like the new norm?

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:23.182
I think the vacated has become

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:23.883
weaponized now.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.585
It's become normalized as an activity.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.820
I never worry about this myself, frankly,

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:30.123
but that was just kind of my own dynamic,

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:31.190
I suppose.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.925
But I think members now

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:34.494
see this as sort of a tool

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:36.362
they can take for a ride.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:38.398
I think that's bad for the institution.

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:40.032
The last thing John Boehner, literally

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:40.566
the last words

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:41.401
John Boehner said to me

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:42.335
as he walked out the door

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:43.836
of his Smoke-Filled office

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.471
that I took over

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:46.439
was don't forget

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:47.440
your number one job

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.875
is to preserve this institution,

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:50.676
defend the institution

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:51.878
and if you're a committee chair,

00:06:51.878 --> 00:06:53.279
which is what I was, I was

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.814
chair of Ways and Means of Time.

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:56.182
I never really thought about that.

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:56.749
Most members

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.951
don't think about the institution. Right.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.653
You're thinking about your policy agenda

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:01.320
or whatever it is

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.588
you want to get done in Congress.

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:03.389
That's not normally

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:04.957
what you're thinking of.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.093
So I became a big institutionalist,

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:08.494
but it took me a little while once

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:09.228
I became speaker

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:10.730
because I realized

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:11.731
just how important

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:12.498
for our freedom

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:13.800
in our liberty, in our democracy.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.401
This is

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.003
talk about your book, American Renewal

00:07:17.003 --> 00:07:17.703
The Conservative Plan

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:19.038
to Strengthen the Social Contract

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:20.873
and Save the Country's Finances.

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:21.641
We are

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:22.975
currently economists

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.877
say whether we're in a recession or not,

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.879
they're definitely headed into something

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:28.781
called a slow session.

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:30.149
This morning, new inflation numbers show

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:31.951
the surging price is fading slightly.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.086
Inflation is still a threat.

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:36.489
What if you were advising

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.056
Kevin McCarthy

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:38.925
in the House Republicans right now

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:40.126
What would you tell them to do

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.794
about the U.S. economy?

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.229
They know what I think.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:45.164
I'm still friends with these guys.

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:46.799
I think look, what

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:47.700
what I say in this book

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.601
is, first, of all,

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:49.469
the best case

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:50.136
for America

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.572
is a really long lost generation,

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:54.273
a stagflation

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:55.341
that is going to last more

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:57.443
than just like a Japanese lost decade.

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:59.846
If we don't get our act together,

00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:01.247
that's the best case scenario.

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:01.881
We're likely

00:08:01.881 --> 00:08:03.249
to have more of a debt crisis,

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.351
which CBO talks about in projects,

00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:06.819
which is really ugly.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.254
That means the social contract

00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:08.921
is unaffordable.

00:08:08.921 --> 00:08:09.388
That means

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:10.890
if you think we're polarized today,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.992
wait till we have a debt crisis.

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.360
So what we're putting in this book

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.495
are all the solutions

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:17.196
we think are necessary

00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:17.864
to make sure

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:18.698
that we can make good

00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:19.732
on our social contract,

00:08:19.732 --> 00:08:21.501
which I would argue

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:22.668
center left and center right.

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:23.636
We agree on.

00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:25.338
We want health and retirement security.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:26.239
We want a safety net.

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:26.973
We believe

00:08:26.973 --> 00:08:28.207
in Medicare and Social Security,

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:29.242
Medicaid.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:31.244
So let's make these things solvent.

00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:32.178
Let's make them work better.

00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:33.613
Let's make them guaranteed

00:08:33.613 --> 00:08:34.847
for the current retirees

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:35.748
and make them solvent

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:36.649
for the next generation

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:38.451
so that they're there when we retire.

00:08:38.451 --> 00:08:40.052
That takes persuasion politics.

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:43.456
So I know that when you were speaker

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:45.458
and Donald Trump was president,

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:47.894
you guys would talk about this

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:49.695
and he did not understand

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:51.230
why you would ever want to do it,

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.065
because it's bad policy. That's right.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:54.767
You just didn't think was ever popular.

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:56.936
And he has

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:58.404
and I'm not blaming this on him,

00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:00.106
but he has certainly empowered

00:09:00.106 --> 00:09:01.340
the kind of populism

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:01.841
you're talking about,

00:09:01.841 --> 00:09:03.809
smash mouth clicks

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.845
with cable hits on Fox, etc.,

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:06.746
where you're a board member.

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:09.248
By the way, he has popularized that.

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:11.450
He is empowered that we saw it last week.

00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:12.952
So how can this be achieved

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:14.387
even within the Republican Party

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:15.888
before you even get to the Democrats?

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:16.556
A couple of things?

00:09:16.556 --> 00:09:18.824
He's fading fast. He's a proven loser.

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:20.426
He cost us the House at 18.

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:21.761
He cost us the White House in 2008,

00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:24.063
cost us the Senate again and again.

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:25.097
And I think we all know that.

00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:26.532
And I think we're moving past Trump.

00:09:26.532 --> 00:09:27.733
I really think that's the case.

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:29.101
I, I can't imagine him

00:09:29.101 --> 00:09:30.436
getting the nomination, frankly.

00:09:30.436 --> 00:09:31.270
And I don't mean this

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:31.804
because I don't want it.

00:09:31.804 --> 00:09:32.672
I don't want to get the nomination.

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:33.439
I just don't think he will

00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:35.041
as an analytical point

00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:36.542
that the thing that

00:09:36.542 --> 00:09:38.010
I that I take solace

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:39.478
in with all the machinations

00:09:39.478 --> 00:09:40.880
you saw last week,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.015
most of that wasn't personal.

00:09:43.015 --> 00:09:44.584
Most of that was around

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:46.085
fiscal responsibility.

00:09:46.085 --> 00:09:47.853
Most of that was about a concern

00:09:47.853 --> 00:09:49.889
about spending, inflation and debt.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:51.390
That's great.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.925
I think you need to persuade the country

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:54.994
as to the solutions and the problem.

00:09:54.994 --> 00:09:55.695
And I don't think

00:09:55.695 --> 00:09:58.097
brinksmanship solves those things.

00:09:58.097 --> 00:10:00.900
But what's behind that is a good thing,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.435
which is Republicans

00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:04.537
finally requiring their moorings

00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:05.905
on the party of fiscal

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:07.440
responsibility and limited government.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.809
That to me is the good thing that I see

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:11.811
in all of this weird stuff.

00:10:11.811 --> 00:10:12.612
That's the good thing.

00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:13.346
So the question is, can

00:10:13.346 --> 00:10:14.981
we put very positive in reform

00:10:14.981 --> 00:10:15.414
in substance?

00:10:15.414 --> 00:10:17.149
Yeah, well, I'm a Republican.

00:10:17.149 --> 00:10:19.752
I am not a member of the organized party.

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:20.720
I'm a Republican.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.622
This very nice little Will Rogers.

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:24.156
So the point I'm trying to make is

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:26.726
those juices are flowing

00:10:26.726 --> 00:10:27.593
and that is churning.

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:28.461
And I think we're

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:29.495
we're hopefully coming back

00:10:29.495 --> 00:10:30.930
full circle to being

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:31.897
maybe a populist party.

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:32.698
That's great and fun.

00:10:32.698 --> 00:10:33.866
I want to be popular,

00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:35.968
but principled and with policy

00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:37.403
is that solve problem with governance?

00:10:37.403 --> 00:10:37.570
Yeah.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:39.271
Fiscal conservatism is is

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:41.474
was a theme that you saw behind

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:42.341
all of those taxes here.

00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:42.908
What's a good thing?

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:43.776
When Congressman Chip Roy

00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:44.310
was on

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.277
State of the Union on Sunday,

00:10:45.277 --> 00:10:46.579
he said we know this

00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:48.047
debt crisis is coming with a debt ceiling

00:10:48.047 --> 00:10:48.681
like hearing that.

00:10:48.681 --> 00:10:51.050
Let's get let's get working on it now.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.351
Right. As opposed to July. Right.

00:10:52.351 --> 00:10:53.619
I don't think you'll fix it

00:10:53.619 --> 00:10:54.620
with a debt crisis,

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.622
but I think you need to go

00:10:56.622 --> 00:10:58.357
persuade the country and offer solutions.

00:10:58.357 --> 00:10:59.892
And having gavels and chairmanships.

00:10:59.892 --> 00:11:01.193
I had two of them.

00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:01.927
That's what you do.

00:11:01.927 --> 00:11:03.462
You build the case in Ways and Means

00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:04.263
in financial services

00:11:04.263 --> 00:11:05.197
and Budget Committee.

00:11:05.197 --> 00:11:06.565
You show the public what

00:11:06.565 --> 00:11:07.400
what's what's going on

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:08.734
and why our solutions are better and why,

00:11:08.734 --> 00:11:09.769
frankly,

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:10.469
if you just

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:11.537
whistle past the graveyard,

00:11:11.537 --> 00:11:12.805
ignore these problems,

00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:14.073
you're hurting Americans,

00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:15.074
you're hurting the social

00:11:15.074 --> 00:11:16.208
your kids are my kids.

00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:17.576
Your social contract. Yes.

00:11:17.576 --> 00:11:19.679
Not just our kids, but seniors. Sure.

00:11:19.712 --> 00:11:20.546
So that's what I meant.

00:11:20.546 --> 00:11:21.781
Like when you and I are seniors,

00:11:21.781 --> 00:11:22.682
that's that's

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:23.883
that's what I was referring to.

00:11:23.883 --> 00:11:25.184
Last quick question.

00:11:25.184 --> 00:11:25.985
Congressman George

00:11:25.985 --> 00:11:27.153
Santos, he's lied about

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.289
apparently everything on his resumé.

00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:30.956
At least

00:11:31.023 --> 00:11:32.058
six House Republicans

00:11:32.058 --> 00:11:32.792
have said he should resign,

00:11:32.792 --> 00:11:34.794
including five New York Republicans.

00:11:34.794 --> 00:11:35.728
Do you agree?

00:11:36.062 --> 00:11:37.129
Sure. Yeah, sure, I agree.

00:11:37.129 --> 00:11:38.431
My guess is they'll probably let

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:39.932
the Ethics Committee run its course.

00:11:39.932 --> 00:11:41.367
It's a fraudulent candidacy.

00:11:41.367 --> 00:11:43.502
This isn't a embellished candidacy

00:11:43.502 --> 00:11:44.904
to fraudulent candidacy.

00:11:44.904 --> 00:11:46.439
He hopes to his voters.

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:47.973
So, of course, he should step down.

00:11:47.973 --> 00:11:48.340
He doesn't

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:49.608
strike me as an honorable person, though.

00:11:49.608 --> 00:11:50.776
I don't know the guy.

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:51.644
So my guess is

00:11:51.644 --> 00:11:52.411
it will probably go through

00:11:52.411 --> 00:11:53.446
the ethics course.

00:11:53.446 --> 00:11:55.281
I can't imagine the guy's going to stay

00:11:55.281 --> 00:11:56.382
very long.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:56.716
All right.

00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:57.917
Former House Speaker Paul Ryan,

00:11:57.917 --> 00:11:58.551
good to see you, sir.

00:11:58.551 --> 00:11:59.652
Thanks for being here. Good to see Jake.

